Description:
The Cork Area Commuter Rail (CACR) programme builds upon the work already carried out by the National Transport Authority (NTA) to enhance the rail offering in the Cork Region through the suburban rail proposals in the Cork Metropolitan Area Transport Strategy (CMATS). Based on the proposals made in CMATS, with a minimum of a 10-minute service interval being the main target, the CACR will be developed firstly in relation to service options and secondly, the required infrastructure interventions required to meet the specified service.
Infrastructure elements of the programme are:
- Signalling improvements;
- New platform at Kent on bypass line for northbound through running;
- Second track between Glounthaune and Midleton;
- Second platform at Cobh station;
- Passing loops at all new stations north of Kent to mitigate impact on Intercity services;
- Additional platform or infrastructure at Mallow;
- Additional rolling stock and staff;
- New or increased capacity rail depot and
- Railway electrification infrastructure including consideration of all forms – Battery Electric Multiple Unit (BEMU), Electric Multiple Unit (EMU), etc.

Proposed stand-alone diagnostic centre on the St Joseph's site under the governance of Beaumont Radiology Dept. The development will consist of;
3 Ultrasound Rooms, 4 X-Ray rooms, Radiology led CT and MRI, Cardiology led CT and MRI & Relevant support services
Proposed Floor Area - 1135sqm.

Construction of 29 km of a new Greenway along the Grand Canal from Sallins to Clonkeen on the Offaly Border. The main components of the works include:               - Widening of the existing trail;
- Resurfacing of the existing trail;
- Improvements to public and private road junctions;
- Construction of 2 pedestrian bridges along the route - one at Landenstown and another before Robertstown; and
- Construction of various ancillary works.
Part 8 planning approval of the new bridges is expected in July 2025. Construction will be delivered in four packages, commencing in Q2/2025 with completion by Q4/2026.

Luas Finglas is an extension of the Luas Green Line, running from Broombridge to Charlestown through Tolka Valley and Finglas village. The proposed route includes: 
- 3.9km in length;
- four new stops: St Helena’s, Finglas Village, St Margaret’s and Charlestown;
- 350-vehicle park and ride facility near the St Margaret’s Road stop;
- interchange opportunities with bus and rail networks and
- Journey time from Charlestown to Trinity College in the city centre of approximately 30 minutes.
